{"from": "artifact:herodian-oil-lamp", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-01-ten-virgins-lamps", "grade": "C", "note": "The Herodian oil lamp, the household clay lamp 'considered to be used mainly by Jews', common from the 1st century BC to the end of the 1st century AD - the object the parable's bridesmaids carry. The evidence attests the lamp custom, never that the parable was told."}
{"from": "artifact:herodian-oil-lamp", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-03-04-oil-in-vessels", "grade": "C", "note": "The Herodian lamp burned oil from a container with a wick (see the oil-lamp source on the claim); the separate carrying-vessels of the wise virgins are not evidenced by the find."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sotah-9-14", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-06-bridegroom-at-night", "grade": "C", "note": "Sotah 9:14 reports that brides went out in a palanquin (aphiryon) and bridegrooms wore crowns and drums played, until the war decrees (Vespasian, Titus, Bar Kokhba) banned them - wedding processions in Judea before c. 132 CE. The mishna is redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe 30 CE practice exactly, and it says nothing of lamps or of the night hour."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-batra-5-9", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-09-oil-sellers", "grade": "B", "note": "Bava Batra 5:9 regulates the sale of food and drink 'that has an established price, such as wine and oil' - oil as a priced retail commodity. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe 30 CE trade exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-metzia-3-11", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-27-bankers-interest", "grade": "C", "note": "Bava Metzia 3:11: one who deposits money with a money changer - if bound the changer may not use it, if unbound 'the money changer may use it' - the money changer as a deposit-taker who works deposited money. Redacted c. 200 CE; it may not describe 30 CE practice exactly, and it names no interest."}
{"from": "artifact:babatha-archive", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-27-bankers-interest", "grade": "C", "note": "The Babatha archive (excavated, Cave of the Letters, 1960) includes documents recording that Judah 'had taken a loan of 60 denarii for a year at 12% interest from a Roman centurion stationed at Ein Gedi' - lending at interest documented in the Judean Desert, though in 128 CE, not 30 CE."}
{"from": "artifact:murabbaat-debt-acknowledgement", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-27-bankers-interest", "grade": "C", "note": "Among the c. 120 Roman-period documents of Wadi Murabba'at is 'a debt acknowledgement' - documented debt in Judea in the decades before the Bar Kokhba revolt. The specific Mur 18 loan of 55/56 CE could not be verified on a fetched page."}
{"from": "text:copper-scroll-3q15", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-18-25-money-buried", "grade": "C", "note": "The Copper Scroll (3Q15, 1st century CE) is 'not a literary work, but a list of 64 places where various items of gold and silver were buried or hidden' - sums of money concealed in the ground in Judea. Whether the treasure existed is disputed; the scroll attests the practice of hiding valuables, not any actual find (node reused from ch 13)."}
{"from": "text:copper-scroll-3q15", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-14-15-talents-money", "grade": "C", "note": "One Copper Scroll entry describes '900 talents (30.05 tons / 868,000 troy ounces) of buried silver' - the talent used in Judea as a unit of very large sums. The reality of the listed treasure is disputed."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-17-320-tribute", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-14-15-talents-money", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 17.318-320 gives the annual tributes of the Herodian realms in talents (200 for Perea and Galilee, 100 for Philip's realm, 600 for Archelaus) - the talent as the unit of large sums in the period (reused from ch 18)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-116-119", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-36-prisons", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus: John the Baptist 'was sent a prisoner, out of Herod's suspicious temper, to Macherus, the castle I before mentioned, and was there put to death' - imprisonment by a Herodian ruler in the period (reused from ch 3). Machaerus is in Perea, not Judea or Galilee proper."}
{"from": "site:machaerus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-36-prisons", "grade": "C", "note": "The Herodian fortress of Machaerus, rebuilt by Herod the Great and held by Antipas, where Josephus reports John the Baptist was held prisoner - the kind of fortified holding place the practice presupposes. The standing ruins are later than 30 CE in part."}
{"from": "text:1-enoch-62-son-of-man", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:mt-25-31-son-of-man-throne", "grade": "C", "note": "1 Enoch 62:5-9 expects 'that Son of Man Sitting on the throne of his glory', before whom kings and the mighty fall down - a pre-Christian Jewish throne-of-glory judgment scene (reused from ch 16 and 19)."}
{"from": "claim:mt-25-31-son-of-man-throne", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:daniel-7-13", "note": "Cross-reference: Daniel 7:13, 'one like a son of man' coming with the clouds of the sky - the scriptural background of the coming Son of Man. A biblical text is not independent evidence."}
